        <!-- SECTION 1: What Basement Floor Paint Does -->
        <div id="why-paint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Basement Floor Paint Actually Does (and Doesn't Do)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Basement floor paint is a film-forming coating — a pigmented acrylic or epoxy-modified acrylic resin that dries into a continuous, semi-flexible layer sitting on top of the concrete. That's a meaningfully different mechanism from a penetrating sealer, which soaks into the slab and hardens it from within, and from a full epoxy coating system, which builds a much thicker, harder film specifically engineered for heavy commercial or garage-level abuse. Basement floor paint sits in the middle of that spectrum: thick enough to add real color and a genuine, if moderate, layer of moisture resistance, but not thick or chemically resistant enough to replace a true waterproofing system on a slab with an active water problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Done right, the payoff is significant. A painted basement floor is easier to sweep and mop than bare concrete, resists the fine chalky dusting that unsealed slabs are notorious for, and instantly makes an unfinished basement look like usable space rather than a construction site. Good <strong>concrete basement floor paint</strong> also blocks a meaningful amount of surface moisture and slows the musty smell that comes from dampness wicking up into stored cardboard, carpet, and furniture. It will not, by itself, stop a basement from flooding, and it will not cure a slab that's actively taking on water from hydrostatic pressure — we cover exactly how to tell the difference in section 5 below.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers Don't Lie</p>
